# rill-deny.rules — Codex execpolicy port of the Rill Tier 3 destructive-op
# denies (see .claude/rules/rill-autonomous-execution.md §6). Installed to
# $vault/.codex/rules/rill-deny.rules by `rill init` / `rill update`.
#
# Syntax: Starlark, evaluated by `codex execpolicy`. The only rule
# constructor is `prefix_rule(pattern=[...], decision=...)`. `decision` is
# "allow" or "forbidden" ("deny" is not a valid keyword). A pattern element
# is either a literal string (exact token match) or a list of strings
# (matches any one of them at that position — used below for remote-name /
# branch-name alternatives). Matching is a **prefix** match over the
# command's argv tokens: a rule matches if argv starts with the pattern,
# regardless of what follows. There is no wildcard token (`*`, `None`, and
# `[]` are all rejected by the parser) — an unlisted token position can only
# be covered by enumerating it. When rules from multiple loaded files
# conflict on the same command, "forbidden" always wins over "allow",
# regardless of file order or which pattern is more specific (verified
# empirically on this machine, codex-cli 0.144.1).
#
# Known residual gaps (inherent to argv-prefix matching, not oversights):
#   - No wildcard for "any remote name": `git push <remote> main/master` is
#     approximated by enumerating the two conventional remote names (origin,
#     upstream). A push to a differently-named remote (e.g. a custom
#     mirror) is not covered.
#   - Position-sensitive: a flag is only matched where the pattern places
#     it. `git push --force origin main` matches; `git push origin main
#     --force` (force reordered to the end) does not, because the pattern
#     requires the exact prefix `git push --force`.
#   - Exact token equality, not substring/glob: `git push --force` does not
#     match `git push --force-with-lease` (different literal token). The
#     analogous Claude-side Bash(...) deny list uses a glob over the whole
#     command string, so it incidentally catches such variants; this
#     execpolicy port, being argv-structured, does not.
#   - Refspec / implicit-branch forms (`git push origin HEAD:main`, or a
#     bare `git push` relying on `push.default=current` while on main) are
#     not covered — the destination branch isn't a standalone token to
#     match against. (The Claude-side glob has the same gap.)
#   - Shell indirection bypasses argv-prefix matching entirely: if the
#     command actually executed is `bash -c "git push --force"`, the first
#     argv token is `bash`, not `git`, so none of these rules see the
#     embedded git invocation. This is a structural limit of any
#     argv-prefix allow/deny mechanism, not specific to this rule set.
#
# The `rill push` CLI wrapper is unaffected by design: these rules only
# match when the agent's own shell command begins with the literal tokens
# `git ...` / `gh ...`; `rill push` has a different argv[0] and never
# matches.

# --- git push --force / -f (overwrites remote history) ---
prefix_rule(pattern=["git", "push", "--force"], decision="forbidden")
prefix_rule(pattern=["git", "push", "-f"], decision="forbidden")

# --- git push <remote> main/master (direct main push; use a PR) ---
prefix_rule(
    pattern=["git", "push", ["origin", "upstream"], ["main", "master"]],
    decision="forbidden",
)

# --- git reset --hard (discards working tree) ---
prefix_rule(pattern=["git", "reset", "--hard"], decision="forbidden")

# --- git clean -f (removes untracked files unrecoverably) ---
prefix_rule(pattern=["git", "clean", "-f"], decision="forbidden")

# --- git branch -D (removes unmerged branch state) ---
prefix_rule(pattern=["git", "branch", "-D"], decision="forbidden")

# --- gh pr merge --admin (bypasses branch protection) ---
prefix_rule(pattern=["gh", "pr", "merge", "--admin"], decision="forbidden")
